         "text": [
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"desc\"><img id=\"imgUniverseTimeBigBang\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('imgUniverseTimeBigBang', '0%')\" src=\"\" class=\"imgcenter\"/><b>Figure 1</b>: The history of the universe, from the Big Bang to today (<htcite0>).</p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "076bf946-5099-493e-8eb3-89d672f9b466",
                     "text": "©ESA",
                     "page": "The Universe Across Space-Time",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2023",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "### The Big Bang\n\nThe picture above shows the history of the universe — from the Big Bang all the way to today. Scientists describe it with a model called Lambda-CDM (<htcite0>).\n\n#### The First Ideas\n\nFor a long time, people believed the universe never changed. They imagined it as completely still, like a statue (<htcite1>).\n\nThen Edwin Hubble (<htcite2>) studied distant galaxies and discovered something surprising: they are moving away from us! The universe is growing. Since the old idea did not match what he could see, scientists had to change it.",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "076bf946-5099-493e-8eb3-89d672f9b466",
                     "text": "©ESA",
                     "page": "The Universe Across Space-Time",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2023",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "11311584-03d0-4c19-a807-28e5aaced8d0",
                     "text": "Kragh, H.",
                     "page": "pp. 5",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2013",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "1af85131-52aa-49ee-8f9b-2409eb806900",
                     "text": "Hubble, Edwin",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "1929",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection: What Would You Do?</b><br /><br />Imagine you are a scientist living in <htdate0>, the year Hubble discovered that the universe was expanding. What would you do?<br /><br />a) You would say that every scientific model is wrong.<br />b) You would do nothing and wait for other people to investigate.<br />c) You would study the new data and try to build better models.</div></div></center></p>",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": [
                  {
                     "type": "gregory",
                     "year": "1929",
                     "month": "-1",
                     "day": "-1"
                  }
               ],
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Different scientists gave different answers — and some ideas even appeared before Hubble’s discovery (<htcite0>):\n- In <htdate0>, Alexander Friedmann showed that Einstein’s equations allowed the universe to be dynamic and expanding.\n- In <htdate1>, Georges Lemaître also proposed that the universe was expanding — though not exactly the way we understand it today.\n\nEven though these works were published before Hubble’s measurements, almost nobody paid attention to them until the <htdate2>s.\n\n#### Two Competing Ideas\n\nOur understanding grew when, in <htdate3>, George Gamow proposed that the universe began as an extremely hot, extremely dense primordial gas (<htcite1>; <htcite2>).\n\nBut not everyone agreed. Another idea, called the steady-state theory, said the universe does expand, yet its average density stays the same — so it would have neither a beginning nor an end (<htcite3>; <htcite4>).\n\nIn an unusual twist, one of these debates gave a name to the model we use today.\n\nOn <htdate4>, during a BBC interview about the steady-state theory, Sir Fred Hoyle said the other model (still without a name) was unacceptable — and called it the “Big Bang” three times. The name stuck (<htcite5>).\n\n### How Do We Choose Between Models?\n\nWhen two models explain the same natural phenomenon, we must find out which one matches reality.\n\nHow? By measuring — that is the only way to know whether a model is correct.\n\nIn <htdate5>, Arno Penzias and Robert Wilson measured the cosmic microwave background radiation: a faint glow left over from the early universe, like an echo of its first light (<htcite6>; <htcite7>).\n\nThese measurements did not fit the steady-state model, but they matched the Big Bang’s predictions perfectly.\n\nThat is why scientists consider the Big Bang model the most accurate explanation of the universe.",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "11311584-03d0-4c19-a807-28e5aaced8d0",
                     "text": "Kragh, H.",
